A||||7||JohnnyK||Battery went completely dead overnight?||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||05-27-2000||05:38 PM||||I have no idea what happened, but I went to start the car this morning, and there was nothing.. No lights, no chime, nothing. I know for sure I did not leave anything on.. I jumped the car and it started fine, but I only let it run for about 1 minute, then I shut it off, disconnected the jumper and tried, and it started... Tried again in 15 minutes, and there was nothing again.. Could this be due to a short or something? ( I remeber when I first got the car, and if I tried to stall it, it would not start up after, and had to sit for a while)... Anyways, the alternator is good, battery is new, so I'm assuming it would be a short? The thing I dont' understand is, it used to be fine if it sat for a while? Quick and easy way to check for shorts?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||05-27-2000||11:11 PM||||Another forum member posted the answer to this awhile back, amazing in its simplicity.<P>Disconnect the positive cable and put a test light in, any shorts will make the light glow.<BR>Now remove fuses until the light goes out, this narrows the search considerably.<P>I remember advice so much better than names, but thanks to the original poster for good help.||||reg||12
Z||000002||JohnnyK||05-28-2000||01:24 AM||||Kinda new at this.. Put the test light in between the positive cable and the battery?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||05-28-2000||02:20 AM||||Yes.  A home made test light can be fabricated out of a flashlight bulb and alligator clips if need be.  <BR>The flashlight bulb won't take twelve volts so don't put full battery on it.  The short in the system should be draining at way less than twelve volts or wires would be smoking.||||reg||12
Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-28-2000||06:36 AM||||dont be so quick about the alt. Ive had a new alternator for a few months and working fine. one day  pulled car out to work in garage. when i went back to put it in was completely dead. alternator had shorted internally in just that few seconds. It does just bask in tons of heat, just inches away from exhaust manifold. Original one fried rite after I steam cleaned the engine. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||05-28-2000||12:21 PM||||I'm pretty sure it's not the alternator because it was fine until I stoped it and went away for awhile.. But isn't there always a drain on the battery that would make the light light up? Like for the clock and such? Lights?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phaeton||05-28-2000||05:45 PM||||Puts their fuse #1 on my list. Unplugging alternator would be #2.  After that  you get to roll the dice.||||reg||12
Z||000007||JohnnyK||05-28-2000||06:33 PM||||But I mean, wouldn't the test light be on all the time anyways because the radio needs a constant supply of power to keep the time and stuff?<BR>||||reg||1
